<<
/Title (A. Working Dataset The dataset used torunand validate this pipeline contains 391 labeled email recordswith five fields: id \(unique identifier\), subject \(email subjectline\), body \(email body text\), email_text \(subject and body combined, used for NLP processing\), and label \(target class: phishing or legitimate\). The class distribution is 229 phishing emails \(58.6%\) and 162 legitimate emails \(41.4%\), a reasonably balanced split for training, shown in Fig. 1.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 313 0 R
/First 384 0 R
/Last 384 0 R
/Count -1
/Next 385 0 R
>>
endobj
356 0 obj
<<
/Title (C. Preprocessing Statistics After cleaning, stopwordremoval, and lemmatization, the TF- IDF vectorizer \(unigramsand bigrams, minimum document frequency of 2\) producedavocabulary of 702 terms from the 391-email corpus. Thisrelatively small, task-specific vocabulary reflects the templated nature of the working dataset; a larger, more heterogeneous real-world corpus would be expected to yield a substantially larger and more diverse vocabulary, which is one reason the results in Section VI should be interpreted as an upper bound rather than a realistic field estimate.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 313 0 R
/Prev 385 0 R
>>
endobj
