388 0 obj
<<
/Title (Beyond raw accuracy,thefourmodels differ in practical deployment characteristics.NaiveBayes trains and predicts fastest and requires theleastmemory, making it attractive for high-throughput mailservers.Logistic Regression offers the best balance of accuracyandinterpretability, as discussed above. Random Forestismorerobust to noisy or non-linear feature interactions butisslowertotrain and produces a larger, less portable model. LinearSVMoffers strong margin-based separation but requiresprobabilitycalibration, as used here, to produce the probabilityscoresneeded for a user-facing verdict. These trade-offs, ratherthanrawaccuracy alone, motivated the choice of Logistic Regressionforthe client-side prototype in Section VII.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 358 0 R
/Prev 399 0 R
>>
endobj
389 0 obj
<<
/Title (bigrams present in the exported vocabulary; \(3\) weight each term by its stored IDF value and L2-normalize the resulting vector; and \(4\) compute the dot product with the exported coefficient vector, add the bias term, and apply the sigmoid function to obtain a probability. This re-implementation was verified against the original Python model on multiple sample emails to confirm numerically identical predictions before deployment.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 359 0 R
/Next 390 0 R
>>
endobj
390 0 obj
<<
/Title (The prototype allows a user to paste any email text and instantly receive a phishing-probability score, a plain-language verdict, and a breakdown of the words that most influenced the decision. All inference runs client-side in the browser, so no email content is transmitted to any server, which is a meaningful privacy advantage over a server-hosted alternative and removes network latency from the prediction loop.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 359 0 R
/Prev 389 0 R
>>
endobj
